Pretty much, yes. Im not saying that it cannot be done. But there is a 
difference of focus which tends to ruin many efforts I have seen. 

In the extremes; 
a multiplayer game has maps so balanced that they are like chess boards 
or mirror images of each other. 
The pieces are exact matches but maybe a different color
there are no random events 
there is no luck involved

A solo-player game has randomly generated maps so extreme that you might 
throw out a few before you find one you want to start on
the pieces are so unique that you find yourself able to play very well 
with on nation set and very badly with another
many extreme random events which force to play with/against the events 
almost as much as any opponents
luck can be a key recognized factor in major turns of the game

Those are extremes! And there are examples of each. There are also many 
games that are somewhere on a scale between the two. And there are a few 
(very few) which while being obviously more one or the other are actually 
quite playable in both modes. I just feel for the poor developer who must 
either face the wrath of the MPers saying that its impossible to get a 
fair game, or the SPers saying that its boring, or both.

> That's my point. The presence or absence of multiplay isn't
